<h1>layer_destroy_instances</h1>
<p>This function can be used to destroy all the instances assigned to the given layer. You supply the layer ID (which you get when you create the layer using <a href="layer_create.htm"><span class="inline">layer_create()</span></a>) or the layer name (as a string - this will have a performance impact), and then all instances that are on the layer will be removed from the game, triggering their <strong>Destroy</strong> and <strong>Clean Up</strong> events.</p>
<p> </p>
<h4>Syntax:</h4>
<p class="code">layer_destroy_instances(layer_id)</p>
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th>Argument</th>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>layer_id</td>
<td><span data-keyref="Type_String"><a target="_blank" href="../../../../GML_Overview/Data_Types.htm">String</a></span> or <span data-keyref="Type_ID_Layer"><a target="_blank" href="layer_get_id.htm">Layer</a></span></td>
<td>The unique ID value of the layer (or the layer name as a string)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p> </p>
<h4>Returns:</h4>
<p class="code"><span data-keyref="Type_Void">N/A</span></p>
<p> </p>
<h4>Example:</h4>
<p class="code">if (global.game_over) <br/>
{<br/>
    layer_destroy_instances(layer);<br/>
}</p>
<p>The above code will check a global variable and if it&#39;s <span class="inline">true</span> then all instances that are on the layer of the calling instance will be destroyed (including the calling instance).</p>
<p> </p>
<p> </p>
<p> </p>
